# Every text file checks out with LF, whatever core.autocrlf says. The
# tools are bash scripts (tools/*.sh, tools/task, tools/zig-cc/*), and a
# Windows checkout with autocrlf=true gave them CRLF, which bash rejects
# ($'\r': command not found). The index was already all LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF in the working tree; PowerShell and cmd
# read either, and editors there expect CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Vendored notices with mixed line endings, kept byte-for-byte.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md -text
